Stop the canonical scan matcher writing one entry past the end of its
neighbour buffer.

The matcher gathers, for each ray, the rays either side of it that belong to
the same cluster, so that it can estimate the surface orientation there. The
buffer it gathers them into is sized at twice the neighbourhood width, which
is right: at most that many either side.

The upward walk honours that. It tests whether the next index is still inside
the neighbourhood before stepping onto it, so it adds at most the
neighbourhood's worth of entries. The downward walk tests the index it is
already standing on instead of the next one, so it takes one step more than
its counterpart and can add one entry beyond what the buffer holds. At the
neighbourhood width the ROS 1 build ships with, twenty, that is a forty first
entry written into a buffer of forty.

The correction mirrors the downward test on the upward one. Nothing else
changes: the same rays are gathered in the same order, and only the step that
was never inside the buffer is not taken.

This matters here rather than being a curiosity because the path is entered
whenever the angle based rejection of correspondences is switched on, and the
ROS 1 build's own matcher settings ship it switched on. So it is the shipped
configuration that overruns, not an unusual corner of it.

Established by running rather than by reading. Address Sanitizer reports a
heap buffer overflow, a write of four bytes zero bytes after a region of one
hundred and sixty, inside the neighbour gathering routine, reached from the
solver's entry point. Without the sanitizer the standard library's own bounds
check fires and the process stops, so on this image the matcher cannot
complete a match at all until this is applied. With it applied, both the
address and the undefined behaviour sanitizers are silent, and five
displacements of a square room, including the zero one, recover to within
1e-15 in all three components.

The same fault is present in the matcher's other variant, where the buffer is
an array in the stack frame rather than one on the heap, so nothing there
complains. Nothing is claimed here about what that write lands on.

This is the Eigen variant of the matcher, on the `csm_eigen` branch, pinned at
commit 04c4a31072e8690bd0ff14de50be20c5b6b43e66, whose sources have not
moved since August 2022. The fault is upstream's rather than this tree's, so
it belongs in an upstream report; until one is made and acted on, this patch
is carried here.

Apply from the root of the matcher's own sources:

  patch -p1 < csm_neighbour_bounds.patch

--- a/src/csm/orientation.cpp
+++ b/src/csm/orientation.cpp
@@ -148,7 +148,7 @@ void find_neighbours(LDP ld, int i, int max_num, std::vector<int>& indexes, size
 		indexes[(*num_found)++] = up;
 	}
 	int down = i; 
-	while ((down >= i-max_num) && (down-1>=0) && ld_valid_ray(ld,down-1) && 
+	while ((down-1 >= i-max_num) && (down-1>=0) && ld_valid_ray(ld,down-1) && 
 			(ld->cluster[down-1] == ld->cluster[i])) {
 		down-=1;
 		indexes[(*num_found)++] = down;
